00:12:16.490 --> 00:12:19.130
and then start the
PostgreSQL auth proxy.

00:12:24.070 --> 00:12:28.090
And finally, you can
connect to the PSQL client.

00:12:28.090 --> 00:12:29.950
And following that,
you can already

00:12:29.950 --> 00:12:31.900
begin to start querying.

00:12:31.900 --> 00:12:33.580
We're going to run
a couple of queries

00:12:33.580 --> 00:12:36.640
similar to earlier before
to show you the connection.

00:12:41.960 --> 00:12:43.460
All right, it looks awesome.

00:12:43.460 --> 00:12:45.230
And we're done.

00:12:45.230 --> 00:12:47.540
Now that you know how to
configure your Cloud SQL

00:12:47.540 --> 00:12:49.490
database, I'll be
sharing with you

00:12:49.490 --> 00:12:52.130
some of the best practices
for cost, as well as

00:12:52.130 --> 00:12:54.050
for performance optimization.

00:12:54.050 --> 00:12:56.300
We will be going through
some of the top tips,

00:12:56.300 --> 00:12:58.520
and I'll also put some
links in the description box

00:12:58.520 --> 00:13:00.950
below for your further reading.

00:13:00.950 --> 00:13:03.650
For performance
optimization, first of all,

00:13:03.650 --> 00:13:07.130
do consider a microservices
architecture, sometimes known

00:13:07.130 --> 00:13:08.990
as vertical sharing.

00:13:08.990 --> 00:13:11.750
This refers to having
multiple smaller databases

00:13:11.750 --> 00:13:13.700
for each microservice
you may have,

00:13:13.700 --> 00:13:16.550
as compared to having one
monolith database for all

00:13:16.550 --> 00:13:19.820
of your services, which can help
you to increase and optimize

00:13:19.820 --> 00:13:21.480
your performance.

00:13:21.480 --> 00:13:24.620
Remember that this is a
general suggestion, and perhaps

00:13:24.620 --> 00:13:27.890
in specific architectures,
a monolithic architecture

00:13:27.890 --> 00:13:29.670
may work for you.

00:13:29.670 --> 00:13:32.180
Secondly, for your
read-heavy workloads,

00:13:32.180 --> 00:13:35.420
do remember to add read
replicas to offload traffic

00:13:35.420 --> 00:13:37.340
from your primary instance.

00:13:37.340 --> 00:13:40.130
Optionally, you can also
make use of a load balancer

00:13:40.130 --> 00:13:44.180
like HAProxy to manage
traffic to your replicas.

00:13:44.180 --> 00:13:47.540
Lastly, use good connection
management practices,

00:13:47.540 --> 00:13:51.350
such as exponential backup
and connection pooling.

00:13:51.350 --> 00:13:53.180
Using these techniques
will help you

00:13:53.180 --> 00:13:56.120
to improve your application's
use of resources

00:13:56.120 --> 00:14:00.140
and also to stay within the
Cloud SQL connection limits.

00:14:00.140 --> 00:14:03.950
As for costs, the Cloud SQL
idle instance recommender

00:14:03.950 --> 00:14:06.980
helps to detect instances
that might be idle.

00:14:06.980 --> 00:14:10.550
It then provides you with
recommendations and insights

00:14:10.550 --> 00:14:12.920
on how you can reduce costs.

00:14:12.920 --> 00:14:16.250
Next, the Cloud SQL over
provisioned instance

00:14:16.250 --> 00:14:18.800
recommender helps
to detect instances

00:14:18.800 --> 00:14:22.160
that are unnecessarily
large for a given workload.

00:14:22.160 --> 00:14:25.190
It then provides you with
some recommendations on how

00:14:25.190 --> 00:14:27.560
you can resize these instances.

00:14:27.560 --> 00:14:30.830
Lastly, do leverage commit
use discounts, which

00:14:30.830 --> 00:14:32.960
are ideal for
predictable workloads

00:14:32.960 --> 00:14:35.750
on a one-year or three-year
basis, which can give you

00:14:35.750 --> 00:14:37.920
significant cost savings.

00:14:37.920 --> 00:14:41.840
Now let's explore some of the
use cases involving Cloud SQL.

00:14:41.840 --> 00:14:43.940
As you can see over
here, Cloud SQL

00:14:43.940 --> 00:14:47.330
works across many use cases,
from gaming to inventory

00:14:47.330 --> 00:14:49.220
management, and many more.

00:14:49.220 --> 00:14:51.720
Next, let's zoom
into one example,

00:14:51.720 --> 00:14:55.040
a sample fictitious gaming
backend database architecture.

00:14:55.040 --> 00:14:58.680
Here, we can see all of the
different databases in action.

00:14:58.680 --> 00:15:02.630
First, we have Cloud Firestore,
a NoSQL document database,

00:15:02.630 --> 00:15:04.880
which can be used for
authentication as well

00:15:04.880 --> 00:15:06.440
as the player database.

00:15:06.440 --> 00:15:09.110
Cloud Memorystore is
then used for storing

00:15:09.110 --> 00:15:12.590
regional, in-memory caches,
which speeds up the access

00:15:12.590 --> 00:15:14.780
to frequently viewed data.

00:15:14.780 --> 00:15:17.780
Cloud SQL or Cloud
Spanner then helps do

00:15:17.780 --> 00:15:19.670
the hosting of the game data.

00:15:19.670 --> 00:15:24.090
You can also log events to
Cloud Bigtable, Google Cloud's

00:15:24.090 --> 00:15:26.150
NoSQL columnar database.

00:15:26.150 --> 00:15:28.820
And finally, you can
also connect to BigQuery

00:15:28.820 --> 00:15:32.360
for data warehousing
and gameplay analytics.

00:15:32.360 --> 00:15:35.340
And if we wanted to take a
look at a real life example,

00:15:35.340 --> 00:15:36.650
we have Krikey.

00:15:36.650 --> 00:15:39.200
Krikey is a mobile
AR gaming startup

00:15:39.200 --> 00:15:42.110
that leverages on Google
Cloud to bring diversity

00:15:42.110 --> 00:15:44.480
into the mobile AR gaming space.

00:15:44.480 --> 00:15:47.520
Krikey migrated to
Cloud SQL and also uses

00:15:47.520 --> 00:15:49.910
some of the other Google
Cloud services, which

00:15:49.910 --> 00:15:52.910
has helped them to reduce their
rendering time for customers

00:15:52.910 --> 00:15:56.090
and also increase the
speed of Krikey games.

00:15:56.090 --> 00:15:58.520
Using Cloud SQL
has helped Krikey

00:15:58.520 --> 00:16:00.770
to decrease the
load time of videos,

00:16:00.770 --> 00:16:03.200
improve the speed
of games, resulting

00:16:03.200 --> 00:16:05.570
in higher app engagement times.

00:16:05.570 --> 00:16:07.940
All in all, I hope that
this video has given you

00:16:07.940 --> 00:16:10.790
a good understanding of
Google Cloud database services

00:16:10.790 --> 00:16:13.850
and how you can get
started on Cloud SQL.

